The difference between OLTP and OLAP comes down to how a database is optimized: OLTP is built to run the daily operations of a business by handling millions of fast,
tiny read/write transactions, while OLAP is built to analyze the business by processing massive, complex queries across historical data.

Technical Breakdown :-
OLTP (Online Transaction Processing): These are operational databases like PostgreSQL or MySQL.
They are heavily normalized (3NF) to ensure data integrity and avoid redundancy.
They write data row-by-row.
When you buy a coffee, an OLTP system instantly locks the row, deducts your balance, updates the store's ledger, and commits the transaction in milliseconds.

OLAP (Online Analytical Processing): These are data warehouses like Snowflake or BigQuery.
They are denormalized (Star or Snowflake schemas) and store data in columns (columnar storage) rather than rows.
When a CEO wants to know 'What was the average coffee sales by region over the last five years?',
an OLAP system can scan billions of rows in seconds because it only reads the 'sales' and 'region' columns, ignoring everything else."

Buisness Context :- 
In a standard data architecture, you never mix the two workloads.
If a data analyst runs a massive aggregation query on the live OLTP database, it will lock the tables and crash the application,
preventing customers from buying things. Instead, we use ETL/ELT pipelines to continuously extract data from the operational OLTP databases
and load it into the analytical OLAP data warehouse where analysts can query it safely.

Design Decision alligned with current tech :-
While the separation of OLTP and OLAP has been the gold standard for decades, I'm closely watching the rise 
of HTAP (Hybrid Transactional/Analytical Processing) databases like TiDB or AlloyDB.
By leveraging intelligent routing and keeping row-based and columnar replicas synced in real-time under the hood,
HTAP systems are attempting to let us run real-time analytics directly on operational data without needing complex, fragile ETL pipelines in between.
